# Done means merged by CI

1. Implement. Run only the one test file that covers the change. Do not run locally what CI runs.
2. One critic pass: `/code-review` on the diff against the real merge base. Fix every real
   finding. Each defect leaves a durable guard behind (a test, a data invariant, a constraint or
   a detector rule) so its class cannot come back.
3. If the change is something an outside caller observes (a status code, a redirect, a rendered
   value, a row count), at least one test asserts it at that boundary. Helper-level unit tests
   cannot; REZ-72 shipped a redirect that never emitted a 308 behind 361 green helper tests.
4. Push the branch, open the PR to `development`, then `gh pr merge --auto --squash`. GitHub
   merges it when every required check is green. Read failures with `gh run view --log-failed`,
   fix, push again.
5. When the work is meant to go live, open `development` to `main` the same way. The push to
   `main` starts Deploy Production, which waits for the founder's approval in the GitHub
   `production` environment. That approval is the one human gate; never trigger the workflow
   yourself.
6. Still founder-only, always: `--apply` against production, a migration on the live database,
   a publish or unpublish. Post the full dry-run output first and wait (AGENTS.md rule 15).
   Approval attaches to the exact mutation set shown; if a re-run of the dry-run differs, it is
   void.
7. Green CI is the definition of merged, not of correct. An epic closes only when the
   buyer-visible symptom is verified gone in production.

